title POSIX is the floor we are leaving
prompt Don't we have to be clear that the microkernel vision of Grain with Caravan is intrinsically non-POSIX? Also search which of those utilities have musl, Rust or Zig implementations already, open source and permissively licensed, so we can prioritize those -- and add a Lindy-first crux-first itinerary for web-fetching the sources into gratitude/ with gratitude writings.
think Keaton is right and it is a framing error in something I shipped four hours earlier, not a nuance. The study wrote granted equals POSIX-mandated, which quietly made POSIX the bedrock of the whole project. It is the bench's floor. Caravan is a root task on seL4's userlevel side -- this tree's own license read settles that -- where there is a syscall interface and no shell, no awk, no grep, no POSIX utility layer at all.
obs Every utility the study counted is a thing that CANNOT EXIST on the machine Grain is being built toward. That does not weaken the measurement, it changes what the measurement is: 1,958 grep sites, 645 sed, 434 awk are not only a portability figure, they are a work estimate for the re-grow.
obs And the tree already knew. external-research/yonder/20260617-201612_useful-utilities.md opens with what we lean on, we eventually re-grow in our own hand, and orders its tiers by how readily each becomes a TAME Rye module -- which is my tier question asked from the far end. Reading it before writing kept this from being a duplicate: it is the elder inventory, and the amendment joins the two lists rather than replacing one.
think The June study already named the C lineage and already chose permissive on purpose -- toybox at 0BSD, sbase and ubase at MIT/ISC, musl at MIT, with busybox named and held at arm's length for GPLv2. What it could not have are the two generations since.
obs uutils/coreutils is Rust and MIT, a hundred utilities, cross-platform to Linux, macOS, BSD, Windows and WASI, and tested against the GNU, toybox and busybox suites -- so its behavioral answers are checkable rather than asserted. ripgrep is MIT and already 992 of our sites. And the Zig row is the one that changes the order: zig-coreutils and zig-utils are MIT, and dawk is a POSIX-compliant awk in Zig.
obs The Zig argument is Lindy rather than taste. Rye compiles through Zig, so a Zig implementation is readable in the language this tree already uses, without a second toolchain between studying a thing and having written it. The shorter that distance, the more likely the re-grow actually happens. That is why dawk is the crux of the fetch order: awk is 434 sites and the hardest of the re-grows, so if it is tractable in Zig the whole base suite is, and if it is not, that is the most valuable early no.
obs Nothing was fetched. A clone is a hand's act and the word is Keaton's; the itinerary is a proposal with its reasons, and each landing carries a written thanks the way gratitude's 75 entries already do -- one page per teacher, a gratitude/utilities/ subfolder once there is more than one, since a room should say what it holds.
obs The clean room did not move. The generations are named with attribution in external-research; the siloed design names only Tally, Caravan, Mantra, Aurora, Rye, Rishi and Glow. Concepts crossed, no code did.
loom amendment posix_correction=1 generations_added=2 permissive_projects=6 fetch_order=5 fetched=0
file external-research/yonder/20260905-064341_what-a-harness-promises-about-its-tools.md the two-horizon correction, the Rust and Zig generations with licenses, and the fetch itinerary
file active-designing/yonder/20260905-064341_the-tools-a-guard-may-assume.md granted means the BENCH guarantees it, and the roster read from the other end is the re-grow list
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the fetch order is a proposal and every clone is your hand; dawk is the crux and the cheapest early no
